<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rules version="5">
    <!--
    ================================================================================================================
    SEARCH RULES v5 (code: net.osmand.search.rules)
    ================================================================================================================

    1. FILES, LAYERS, KEYS
       rules.xml (this base) -> rules_<language>.xml -> rules_<language>_<COUNTRY>.xml, from general to specific.
       The locale is the locale of the DATA, never of the user interface:
         - a map: the longest prefix of its file name in <locales> ("canada" -> en_CA, "canada_quebec" -> fr_CA);
         - a name tag of another language (OBF writer only): name:de on an Italian map -> de_IT.
       Every rule has a key. A lower layer with the key of an upper rule replaces that rule WHOLE (all attributes and
       forms: copy them all when only one attribute has to change), enabled="false" removes it, two rules with one
       key in one file are an error. Keys:
         <query><rule>     the word of from
         <index><rule>     object + from
         <unglue>          glue
         <class0/1/2>      the word
         <skipPenalty>     the word + object
       A stored name keeps the words of OSM: no rule rewrites it. <index> and <poi> ADD names or tags to the OBF,
       <query> adds forms to the words of a query.

    2. WHO APPLIES WHAT

       section    applied by   when                     locale                               data source
       <locales>  both         loading                  only in rules.xml                    map file name
       <index>    OBF writer   name index generation    <unglue>: of the map,                names of objects
                                                        <rule>: of the name tag
       <poi>      OBF writer   POI generation           of the map                           POI names, OSM tags
       <query>    search       every query              of every OBF being read              words of the query

       A change of <index> or <poi> needs a new OBF; a change of <query> works with the OBFs already downloaded.

       Owners (attribute object: a comma list; "*" or no object is every owner):
         street     a street and a building (an address carries the name of its street)
         poi        a POI: its name and its ref
         locality   a city, town, village, hamlet, suburb
         boundary   an administrative boundary
         postcode   a postcode
         building   only in <query>: a part of a house number, see 4.2

    3. <index>: ALTERNATIVE NAMES AND WORD CLASSES (OBF writer)

       Data source: name (locale of the map), name:<lang> and the other names of an object (alt_name, old_name...,
       locale of their language), the English or transliterated name of a POI ("en"). For each of them the writer
       may add an alternative name: its new words become keys of the name index, marked as a rule spelling; the
       search finds the object by them and shows the stored name.

       3.1 <unglue glue="." script="Latin" minPart="2"/>
           Splits words glued by one character: "Mak.by" -> "Mak by", "L'Atelier d'Anaïs" -> "Atelier Anaïs".
           Locale: of the map, for every name of the object. Only words without digits ("1.5" stays), only words
           of the script when script is set; a part shorter than minPart (default 2) is dropped ("d'", "L'").
           Use it for a punctuation glue that users type split. A number glued to a word ("56ave") is a <rule>,
           see 3.2.

       3.2 <rule object="street" from="(?iu)regexp" to="form" mode="Single|All" keys="stats|always"/>
           A Java regexp over the whole name of an owner, to is the replacement ($1 for groups): "Hauptstraße" ->
           "Hauptstr", "Strada Statale 42" -> "SS42". mode="Single" (default) applies only when the regexp matches
           once, "All" replaces every match; keys="always" makes the new words keys whatever their statistics.
           Locale: of the name tag, one form only. Use it for a glued, phrase or tokenizer-lost spelling that a
           one-word query form cannot give. An abbreviation of one word belongs to <query>: no new OBF is needed.
           A glued spelling: Canada Post writes "10720 56 AVE NW", users type "10720 56ave" (#26691), so
           rules_en_CA.xml makes "56Avenue NW", "56Ave NW", "56Av NW" of "56 Avenue NW". A query word matches a word
           of a name whole, so every spelling is a rule of its own, and each one costs a key per matching name.
           A glued form must not be another word: "1 Street" -> "1St" would be the ordinal "1st" (21, 31... too),
           the regexp skips such numbers.

       3.3 <class0>, <class1>, <class2>
           Words that are always a key / service words / frequent words of the locale, over the classes of
           common_words_groups.tsv. A word of an alternative name is a key by its own class.

    4. <query>: FORMS OF A QUERY WORD (search)

       Data source: the words of the query, one token each. A form is one word (the first word of to).

       4.1 Locale: the search reads several OBFs at once and each OBF has its locale. A token gets the forms of
           every locale lazily and matches the names of a map only with the forms of that map: "Pl" is "Place" on
           an English map and stays "Pl" on a German one. The language of the user does not matter.

       4.2 Syntax:
           <rule from="pl" to="Place" object="street"/>      one meaning
           <rule from="st">                                   several meanings, the first one is the main one
               <to object="street">Street</to>
               <to>Saint</to>
           </rule>
           <rule from="apt" object="building"/>               no to: a part of a house number ("apt", "bis")
           <rule from="the" to=""/>                           an ignorable word: an optional token, never a
                                                              penalty, for every owner
           <skipPenalty object="street">de la</skipPenalty>   words of NAMES that do not penalize an object of the
                                                              owners when the query does not have them

       4.3 Filter by object: a form with an object matches only names of its owners (2), checked when a name is
           compared: "pl" -> "Place" finds the street "Place Vendôme", never a POI named "Place". A form without
           object matches every owner. Reading the keys of the name index uses every form (a key has no owner), the
           owner is checked on the name afterwards.

       4.4 Direct and reverse form: every form gets a generated reverse form (to -> from) of the same object.
             direct  (from -> to): the query has the abbreviation, the name the full word: "st" finds "Main Street";
             reverse (to -> from): the query has the full word, the name keeps the abbreviation of OSM:
                                   "street" finds "E Main St", "avenue" finds "56 Ave NW".
           A reverse form is ONE step: "saint" leads to "st", never to the other forms of "st" ("Saint Louis" does
           not become "Louis Street"). A house-number part and an ignorable word have no reverse form.

    5. <poi>: POI NAMES AND TAGS (OBF writer, locale of the map)

       5.1 <abbr short="RCMP" full="Royal Canadian Mounted Police"/>
           Data source: the NAME of a POI. A name with one form gets the other one as an alternative name, both
           ways, whole words, any case, the abbreviation with its dot: "RCMP Detachment" is found by "Royal Canadian
           Mounted Police" and the other way round. POIs only: a street abbreviation is a rule of <query>.
           Use it for a well-known abbreviation of a POI name, usually a phrase that one-word query forms cannot give.

       5.2 <service word="DMV" tags="office=government + government=transportation | amenity=..." name="regexp"/>
           Data source: the OSM TAGS of a POI (sets split by |, the tags of one set joined by +) and, when name is
           set, a regexp over its name. A matching POI gets the tag service_abbreviation=<word> (top_index of
           poi_types.xml): the query "DMV" finds the offices of the service even when their names lack the word.
           Use it for a national service or network known by its abbreviation; the file of the country is the
           country of the service (rules_en_US.xml: DMV, rules_en_CA.xml: RCMP).

       <abbr> or <service>: <abbr> works on the words of a name and finds only POIs whose names already have one of
       the two forms; <service> works on the tags and finds the POIs of the service whatever their names, by the word.

       5.3 <brand value="Aster" tags="amenity=pharmacy"/>: read by the brand analyzer, indexed even below its limits.
    ================================================================================================================
    -->
